{
  "_what": "How a person's typed words fill a screen's inputs without a model (src/input-parsers.mjs). A tool's input_schema property says which parser reads it: its own `x-parse` (a parser name below), else its JSON Schema `format` through `by_format`, else a fixed list of values (`enum`) through `enum_parser`, else its `type` through `by_type`; a plain text input is filled with the words nothing else took when it is the only one (`text_parser`). Every word a parser knows is a row here, so a new language, unit or currency is a row, never code. `leftover_skip` are the words dropped from the start and end of what is left for a text input (\"remind me to call Sam\" leaves \"call Sam\"). `numeric_date_order` reads 10/24 as month/day (`mdy`) or day/month (`dmy`). `bare_hours_after_noon_below`: an hour said with `at` and no am/pm below this (at 5) is read as afternoon. `order` is which parser takes its words first, the most particular first, so \"25 min\" is a duration before it is a number. `default_time` is the time a date-and-time input takes when only a day is said. `duration_unit_of_number`: the unit a number input holds a duration in when nothing names it (a property named after a unit, such as `minutes`, holds that unit).",
